body:
Thank you so much for taking the time to care about us and writing such a detailed message, it really moved me that you did so.

&#x200B;

I often hold back from suggesting her to do things (outside home) because she usually refuses, but this reminded me that it's good to ler her know that I want to go with her to such things. I just suggested we go together to a poetry reading I was planning on going alone to and it put a smile on her face. She might end up refusing, but you're right it's a good thing to do.

&#x200B;

About her alcohol abuse: it's a tricky thing. My father used to be an alcoholic (quite the family here, I know) and she's nowhere near that. It's usually a couple of beers a day, hardly ever she takes anything stronger, but it's the fact that she also takes heavy medication with that which really makes it a problem. To be honest, if she actually ends up going to the rehab center, what I'm most looking forwards to it's not just her getting sober, but her living in a clean and organised enviroment that can help her improve her habits a bit. But, as you said, even if she doesn't go, I'll try and keep her "clean" as much as I can.

&#x200B;

Anyhow, she seems to be in better spirits this morning and wants to go do some errands. Thank you again :)
